-- task-graph/hyperactivity.cub — this repository's task graph (lib/task-graph.cub; its meaning and rules
-- are “The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). The product, Avik's four goals and the one dependency between them
-- (2026-11-13). A goal with no children is one coarse task described by its statement; it is split
-- only when part of it is being tackled immediately (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). Known defects
-- and gaps found in the code are dated `finding (date):` comments at their sites.
--
import "../lib/task-graph.cub"
import "../docs/onboarding.cub"
-- the-product (2026-11-13, Avik): "the product is every screened device in the world simply runs
-- hyperactive kernel. the term "application" becomes fully concrete - does not refer to a silo, but
-- literally to a particular application of computing." "*all* of this is to serve *product*." The
-- root: what Avik wants (“The product” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)).
-- Known (2026-11-16, Avik): the work is not fundamental research into reality; it is a fundamental
-- exposition into ultimate reality, facilitating arbitrary research into concrete and abstract
-- realities: it enables fundamental research into reality (docs/information-acts-and-common-ground.md).
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): the elementary contribution is not one language but what it lets one do:
-- an entire organization (his example: all of Citadel Securities, its trading fleet, research
-- infrastructure and human resources) is a single program with end-to-end machine-verifiability of
-- any provable property, the weakest way to say it supports computational reasoning over the entire
-- information space; and a contribution on rationality: what is amenable to rule-based logic
-- (docs/information-acts-and-common-ground.md, his words).
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): this is a technological work determined by the science of human
-- happiness (he studied with Dacher Keltner; docs/information-acts-and-common-ground.md).
the-product : Node
the-product = node "The product: every screened device in the world runs Hyperactive." nil
-- language-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "complete implementation of hyperactive programming
-- language & features as specified by the paper ... get to the point i can show someone the paper
-- and correctly state "i have implemented this fully correctly" (obviously, that doesn't include
-- constructions about random examples about trading systems or message protocols)". The
-- initial high-priority deliverable.
-- Expanded 2026-11-14, when work began on typed codes: its children are typed codes and
-- normalization by evaluation (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”), in progress,
-- and everything else the paper specifies for the language. What is known is in each child's
-- statement.
-- Which paper: the paper is one value of the language, `hyperactivity` (docs/hyperactivity.cub over
-- lib/paper.cub), version 32, 15 November 2026: its one source (2026-11-15, the-paper-is-one-value).
-- Earlier versions are history, kept by git: v31 (docs/hyperactivity_v31.pdf, 14 November 2026,
-- SHA-256 9c480995e7fb…) and the 11 November copy (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, SHA-256
-- e8f358b4cd2c…). A citation made before 2026-11-15 names the version it read by path, date and
-- digest prefix and stays exact: `git log --all -- PATH` finds that version. Part I of the value is
-- v31's text, so a section number of v31 is the value's.
language-implementation : Node
language-implementation = node "The Hyperactive language and its features, implemented completely and correctly as the paper specifies." (cons the-product nil)
-- typed-codes-and-normalization (2026-11-14): docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”:
-- the language's terms as typed
-- objects in it, Code(Γ, A) = Σ c. Der(Γ, c, A), normalization by evaluation with its soundness
-- equation, eq. (6.5) “reifySound” of that section, and a checker that returns typing derivations. The plan, step by step, is
-- lib/nbe.cub's head; the working file is experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub, swapped into lib/nbe.cub
-- when done. Expanded 2026-11-14, when its step F2d (the interval normalizer's soundness) began.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The language's terms are typed
-- objects in it, with normalization and its soundness, and the checker returns typing derivations
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”). The language's terms as typed objects in it,
-- normalization by
-- evaluation with its soundness, and a checker that returns typing derivations (lib/check.cub is an
-- untyped term with no statement of what it accepts; lib/std.cub's primitives give typed programs
-- its operations at asserted types). In progress in experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub; what remains is in
-- lib/nbe.cub's head (F2d the interval normalizer's soundness, F2d' its canonicity, F2e naturality in substitutions, fund, normalize, the swap), then Kan operations, Glue,
-- universes and object-level data types. This is the easiest language to build it in, not a hard
-- one (2026-11-13, worked out with Avik): it is normalization for a cubical type theory written
-- inside a cubical type theory. Anywhere else (Agda without its cubical mode, Coq, Lean) one first
-- builds a model of cubical sets (presheaves, Kan structure, Glue, univalence); here the model is
-- the host: object paths are host paths, object composition is the host's composition, Glue and
-- univalence are the host's; meanings are host terms, so soundness holds by construction
-- (reifySound is refl) and only readback needs designing. The method for the Kan operations, Glue
-- and universes is published (Sterling and Angiuli, LICS 2021; Cubical Agda, cooltt). Open, and
-- deciding how much of the remaining work is needed: the host identifies interval expressions up to
-- the De Morgan laws judgmentally, yet F2 (the interval's laws, steps F2a to F2e of lib/nbe.cub's head) re-proves every law as a path, because a context's
-- dimensions are encoded as a cube built by recursion on their number, so at a variable number
-- every operation is a stuck recursion. Answered 2026-11-13 (the question is the standard
-- semantics, not one for Avik): in CCHM a term in Γ; Ψ; φ means a family natural in the stage, an
-- element of A at every Ψ′ with a substitution f : Ψ′ → Ψ satisfying φ, commuting with every
-- further substitution; a dimension's value at a stage is an element of the free De Morgan algebra
-- on that stage's names, and the substitutions are maps of that algebra (the cubical-set model). So
-- the number of dimensions is variable only in the meta-level quantification over stages, and a
-- semantic action of interval expressions defined on syntax must be shown to respect the algebra's
-- equations once: that is the well-definedness of the cube category's action, not an artefact of
-- the encoding. p26-wip.cub is that model built inside the host: stages are host cubes (Cube n),
-- interval expressions are data (IEx, DNF), substitutions are Sub and cmap-d; F2a-F2c (every De
-- Morgan law, no 2-cell) is the well-definedness, F2d the normalizer's soundness, F2d' its
-- canonicity, F2e naturality in substitutions. Holding an object dimension as a host interval
-- variable is not available at a variable number of dimensions (the host's interval is not a
-- type: `I × Nat` is refused, checked 2026-11-13), and would move these obligations, not remove
-- them. Decided: p26-wip.cub is kept and continues at F2d. The elaborator's two memory blowups met on the way are below. The longest chain of the project: it
-- should never stop.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub): elaboration: checking a path λ (λ i → sub-
-- pull … keep-S (P i)) whose body applies a defined dependent path P (carry-uniq of a pcat-d)
-- exhausts memory (killed at 5.5 GB; keep-lift's first step, before cong1): the λ's endpoints are
-- computed by evaluating the body at 0 and 1, which unfolds P's definition instead of reading P's
-- endpoints off its type; the same path given to cong1 checks in 2.7M steps. Not yet known: which
-- evaluation step unfolds it. keep-lift's parts: the cubes of contexts its base paths pass through
-- (kl-k2, kl-k3), the meanings over them (kl-E0 … kl-ER0), the dependent paths over each base path
-- (kl-DE, kl-Dr2, kl-D, kl-DR) and the four steps (kl-s1 … kl-s4)
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/std.cub): needs-work. The primitives below give typed programs (the
-- specializer, the translation to machine code) the untyped checker's own operations at types: a
-- definition's code, quotation, normalization, conversion at a type, constructor tables, rewriting.
-- They are trusted, as the checker is, and their types are asserted, not checked. Typed codes
-- replace them by terms whose types are checked.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/check.cub): needs-work. The checker is an untyped term with no
-- statement of what it accepts; it is trusted. The paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”, "Checked syntax and its interpretation" and "Typed stages and checking"): the theorem that it
-- accepts exactly the derivable judgments is separate from its definition. A checker returning a typing derivation
-- (Der) or a refusal is sound by construction; deciding derivability is then its completeness
-- (this node).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/check.cub): needs-work. Solving one implicit argument exhausted the
-- machine's memory (7 GB, killed, exit 137): lib/nbe.cub's `reify-code s K D Δ δ x cx = (fst (fst
-- (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx) , x , embed (snd (fst (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx)))`, where embed's implicit index
-- {KJ} is to be found from its argument's type, at a variable shape s. The same term through a
-- definition with a declared type (nf-code Δ (s , (K , δ , D)) x (fst (rr s) K D Δ δ x cx)) checks
-- in a few thousand steps. Not yet known whether the cost is here (inferring the argument's type by
-- evaluating rr's clauses at the variable s, rather than reading rr's signature) or in the
-- elaborator's syntactic solving. A spine with holes (implicit arguments) is solved where it
-- stands: the head's type is walked along the arguments, a hole standing as a fresh variable, each
-- explicit argument's inferred type matched against its domain, then the result type against the
-- expected one; the universe of a solved type gives its level. The solutions are returned as codes,
-- (elab ...), and the term with them is checked again. An argument's own holes are solved first.
-- The construction (2026-11-15, derived with Avik; this note supersedes every plan for this goal
-- written before it). Scope, from §6.1's own text: the object language is Π, Σ, PathP and a base type
-- (Bool); the paper fixes reify and reflect at Π, Σ and PathP, normalize = reify ∘ eval ∘
-- reflectContext, the fundamental lemma by induction on typed syntax, and (6.5) eval(reify v, ρ) =
-- denote(v, ρ). Composition, Glue and universes are interpreted by the model's own Kan operations and
-- are not normalized: §6.1 says its clauses describe that fragment. Normalizing them is not this goal.
-- Why dimensions are cube directions (forced in CCHM): reifying a path is pathLam(i. reify(pathApp(p,
-- i))), whose code mentions i as a variable of the code. If an object dimension were a host interval,
-- reification pointwise would be a host family of codes indexed by the interval, and every path in
-- the data type Code is constant, so the code could not depend on i. And an environment cannot hold a
-- host interval (I is not a type). So an object dimension is a name in the context, read as a
-- direction of the cube the meanings live over: a context with n dimensions means an n-fold cube of
-- environments, a term a cube over it (the cubical-set model, stages as host cubes).
-- What that forces, and nothing more:
-- 1. The Kripke normalizer for Bool, Π and Σ: meanings host functions of the environment, computability
--    over renamings, the fundamental lemma over semantic substitutions, normalize, reifySound and
--    normalize-sound by refl. This is lib/nbe.cub lines 757-1260 as it stands (about 500 lines).
-- 2. Dimension entries in contexts and cube meanings; path application at an interval expression on
--    cubes by faces, reversal, connections, exchange and diagonal (lib/nbe.cub's cube section).
-- 3. The fundamental lemma over substitutions that send dimensions to interval expressions, not only
--    renamings: the path λ's case gives its body's computability at every point of the interval, which
--    is substitution of an expression for the bound dimension.
-- 4. Hence the one lemma with content: substitution by expressions commutes with path application at
--    expressions (F2e, cmap-papp-ex, checked in experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub, commit f71ad86). Every
--    other case is definitions plus this lemma once.
-- 5. Reflection and reification at PathP: a neutral path applied at a non-constant expression is
--    neutral; at 0 or 1 it is the type's endpoint.
-- Not forced: canonical forms of interval expressions (6.5 asks that readback be sound, not that
-- normal forms be unique; a neutral path application keeps its expression), and every node below this
-- one that normalizes composition, Glue or universes. Then: the checker that returns Der, the
-- substitution law ⟦t[σ]⟧ρ = ⟦t⟧⟦σ⟧ρ, and the specializer law with projections (6.6) and (6.7).
-- Status (2026-11-15): items 1-5, normalize with (6.5), the substitution law and the specializer law
-- with (6.6) and (6.7) are lib/nbe.cub (9415 lines; with experiments/nbe/examples.cub, 1527 passed, 0 failed);
-- experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub and papp-subst-draft.cub are retired into it. Open: the checker.
-- The checker (derived 2026-11-15). Bidirectional over Der as it stands: check mode for λ, pair, path
-- λ, true and false against a type's meaning; inference for variables, application, projections,
-- case and path application; types inferred. Case carries its motive in its code (done: without it,
-- checking a case needs a motive P with P x the given type, higher-order unification, and one code
-- has derivations at motives agreeing at true and false but not at x). Der's binder premises live
-- in contexts built from the conclusion's meaning (ext-h at the family, lam-ctx at the λ), so each
-- binder case builds the conclusion's meaning from the premise's by abstraction over the extended
-- cube, and carries the premise's derivation across the path of contexts that dcube-map fusion gives
-- (comp over Der, as der-tr; done: abs-mean, der-ext). One1 is the singleton of Bool in Type (done), so
-- Bool types are equal by contraction. Conversion is needed where an inferred term is checked and,
-- for every path λ checked against a PathP, between its body's faces and the type's endpoints, even
-- on normal forms. Sound conversion of term meanings is completeness of normalization: from equal
-- normal codes conclude equal meanings. (6.5) holds by refl because a typed code carries its
-- meaning, so this converse is a theorem of its own: neither a checker run on normal forms nor
-- reification gives it, since a computability witness is not unique for a meaning until it is
-- proved. It is a Kripke relation between two meanings, proved by induction on Der like fund
-- (Abel, Coquand and Dybjer's PER model for decidability of conversion).
-- What elab needs besides (derived 2026-11-15): (a) values typed by a type known only up to a path
-- (an argument against a domain, a pair's components, case's branches against the motive at true
-- and false, a PathP's endpoints against its line's faces) are transported pointwise, by a cube of
-- paths over the base mapped by the pair of cubes (so each vertex's path starts at that vertex's
-- value judgmentally), then dcube-pull; (b) check mode takes the expected type with its derivation
-- (a pair's codomain premise and a λ's domain premise need codes), and inference returns the type's
-- derivation with the term's: a variable's from TCtx, weakened; an application's result type by
-- reading the Π type's codomain off its computability (fund, then rr at the codomain), with no
-- inversion of Der; (c) path application parses its interval code to a canonical form and checks
-- dnf-code of it equal to the code (code-dec), canonical and non-constant (dnf-canon, dnf-nonconst),
-- 0 and 1 by dpapp0 and dpapp1. Done so far: code-dec, abs-mean/der-ext, dcube-contr/mean1-path.
typed-codes-and-normalization : Node
typed-codes-and-normalization = node "Typed codes, normalization by evaluation with its soundness, and a checker that returns typing derivations, as §6.1 of the paper (docs/hyperactivity.cub) specifies." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- interval-normal-forms-mean-their-expressions (2026-11-14): F2d of lib/nbe.cub's head. Path
-- application at a normal form of the interval is path application at the form read as an
-- expression (papp-dd f = papp-ex (dnf-ix f)), so the normalizer's join, meet and reversal must
-- mean the expressions' join, meet and reversal: IEq at every skeleton between dnf-ix (dnf-join g
-- h) and ixjoin (dnf-ix g) (dnf-ix h), and likewise for dnf-meet and dnf-neg. No canonicity
-- hypothesis is needed (F2d' is canonicity). The plan, lemma by lemma, is lib/nbe.cub's head; the
-- definitions go after the F2c laws (the De Morgan laws as paths) in experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub.
-- Resolved 2026-11-14: dnf-join-sound, dnf-meet-sound and dnf-neg-sound in
-- experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub with their lemmas, each a chain of the F2c laws, no canonicity
-- hypothesis; the file checks fresh (CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1): 1150 passed, 0 failed, 687M steps, 7.5
-- min.
interval-normal-forms-mean-their-expressions : Node
interval-normal-forms-mean-their-expressions = node "The interval normalizer's join, meet and reversal mean the interval's join, meet and reversal: path application at the normalized form equals path application at the operation on the forms' readings (F2d)." (cons typed-codes-and-normalization nil)
-- typed-codes-remainder (2026-11-14; expanded the same day, when F2d' began): what typed codes need
-- after F2d (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”), in lib/nbe.cub's
-- head's order: the normalizer's canonicity (F2d'), naturality in substitutions (F2e), papp-subst,
-- the fundamental lemma, reflect-env, normalize and its soundness by refl, the swap of
-- experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub into lib/nbe.cub with the examples rewritten; then the Kan
-- operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types.
typed-codes-remainder : Node
typed-codes-remainder = node "Everything else typed codes and normalization by evaluation need after the interval normalizer's soundness (F2d): canonicity, naturality in substitutions, the fundamental lemma, normalize and its soundness, the swap into lib/nbe.cub, then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types." (cons typed-codes-and-normalization nil)
-- normal-forms-are-canonical (2026-11-14): F2d' of lib/nbe.cub's head. npapp and dpapp take a form
-- f with isTrue (dnf-canon f) and isTrue (dnf-nonconst f), and F2e's substitution makes forms by
-- dnf-join, dnf-meet and dnf-neg of canonical forms, so: dnf-norm of any form is canonical; the
-- join, meet and negation of canonical forms are canonical; a canonical form that is not non-
-- constant is lnil or dnf-one. Built on an invariant dnf-ok (each conjunction pairwise lit-lt
-- sorted, the form pairwise conj-lt sorted, an antichain under conj-sub) that every normalizer
-- operation keeps and on which dnf-norm is the identity. The plan, lemma by lemma, is lib/nbe.cub's
-- head.
-- Resolved 2026-11-14: canon-norm, canon-join, canon-meet, canon-neg and const-cases in
-- experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub, canonical being exactly the invariant dnf-ok; the file checks fresh
-- (CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1): 1247 passed, 0 failed, 693M steps, 7.6 min.
normal-forms-are-canonical : Node
normal-forms-are-canonical = node "The interval normalizer's outputs are canonical: normalizing any form, and joining, meeting or negating canonical forms, gives a form its normalization leaves unchanged, and such a form is constant only as 0 or 1 (F2d')." (cons typed-codes-remainder nil)
-- typed-codes-after-canonicity (2026-11-14; expanded the same day, when F2e began): what typed codes
-- need after F2d' (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”), in
-- lib/nbe.cub's head's order: naturality in substitutions (F2e), papp-subst, the fundamental lemma,
-- reflect-env, normalize and its soundness by refl, the swap of experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub into
-- lib/nbe.cub with the examples rewritten; then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-
-- level data types.
typed-codes-after-canonicity : Node
typed-codes-after-canonicity = node "Everything typed codes and normalization by evaluation need after the normalizer's canonicity (F2d'): naturality in substitutions, the fundamental lemma, normalize and its soundness, the swap into lib/nbe.cub, then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types." (cons typed-codes-remainder nil)
-- substitution-commutes-with-path-application (2026-11-14): F2e of lib/nbe.cub's head. fund at
-- dpapp under a substitution whose dimension part sends dimensions to forms needs path application
-- at the substituted form to be the substitution of the path application. The renaming case exists
-- (papp-ex-cmap); this generalizes it to substitution by forms, the crux being the mixed lemmas
-- (path application at a form in a cube's base dimensions against papp-ex in its fibre). The plan
-- is lib/nbe.cub's head.
substitution-commutes-with-path-application : Node
substitution-commutes-with-path-application = node "Substituting forms for dimensions commutes with path application: path application at the substituted expression of the substituted cube is the substitution of the path application (F2e)." (cons typed-codes-after-canonicity nil)
-- typed-codes-after-naturality (2026-11-14): what typed codes need after F2e
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”): papp-subst, the
-- fundamental lemma, reflect-env, normalize and its soundness by refl, the swap of
-- experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub into lib/nbe.cub with the examples rewritten; then the Kan
-- operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types.
typed-codes-after-naturality : Node
typed-codes-after-naturality = node "Everything typed codes and normalization by evaluation need after naturality in substitutions (F2e): the fundamental lemma, normalize and its soundness, the swap into lib/nbe.cub, then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types." (cons typed-codes-after-canonicity nil)
-- path-application-under-substitution-at-forms (2026-11-14): papp-subst, the first step after F2e
-- in lib/nbe.cub's head ("Next, papp-subst"): path application at canonical forms commutes with a
-- substitution's action on meanings, which the fundamental lemma uses at dpapp. Step 1 (dnf-subst
-- and cmap-papp-ddS) is checked in experiments/nbe/papp-subst-draft.cub (to move into p26-wip.cub); step 2 follows
-- subM-papp's route.
path-application-under-substitution-at-forms : Node
path-application-under-substitution-at-forms = node "Path application at canonical forms commutes with the action of a substitution by forms on meanings (papp-subst)." (cons typed-codes-after-naturality nil)
-- typed-codes-after-papp-subst (2026-11-14): after papp-subst: the fundamental lemma, reflect-env,
-- normalize and its soundness by refl, the swap of experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub into lib/nbe.cub
-- with the examples rewritten; then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-level data
-- types.
typed-codes-after-papp-subst : Node
typed-codes-after-papp-subst = node "Everything typed codes and normalization by evaluation need after papp-subst: the fundamental lemma, normalize and its soundness, the swap into lib/nbe.cub, then the Kan operations, Glue, universes and object-level data types." (cons typed-codes-after-naturality nil)
-- language-implementation-remainder (2026-11-14): everything the paper specifies for the language
-- that typed codes and normalization by evaluation do not cover. What is known about it follows
-- (moved here from the language goal's statement when the goal was expanded).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The implementation is shown to be
-- exactly the published calculus, rule by rule, and every deviation is fixed or justified. nobody
-- has compared the implementation with the published calculus, so whether it is "implemented fully
-- correctly" is unknown. Read the evaluator's rule cases (src/net.c), the translation of terms into
-- its graph (src/lang.c), the composition code generated per data type as unchecked text
-- (src/lang.c), universe composition (lib/prelude.cub, %hcompU, Cubical Agda's design, not CCHM's
-- Glue) and the checker's typing clauses (lib/check.cub) against CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber,
-- Mörtberg) and the clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi), recording each rule as
-- matching or deviating, then fix or justify each deviation. What is known follows. The deliverable
-- is not an agent's statement that the code is right: it is the trusted part made small enough to
-- be plainly the published rules, each rule case shown beside the rule it implements, everything
-- else checked or unable to change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work. For every data declaration this generates, as
-- untyped source text, the type's composition (%comp-D), its homogeneous composition, and transport
-- on each constructor and on formal compositions. That text is never checked: it is trusted as
-- CCHM's rules for the declared type, written once in C per declaration rather than as the single
-- schematic rule CCHM gives for inductive and higher inductive types. Show it is exactly that rule,
-- or replace the per-declaration generation by the schematic rule.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work. The calculus's rules are the cases of this function
-- (application, projections, path application, composition, Glue, interval and faces, systems,
-- higher-constructor boundaries, hcomp, transport, case: about 650 lines), the face algebra
-- (src/dnf.c), the composition clauses for the type formers (lib/prelude.cub) and the composition
-- code generated per data type (generated in src/lang.c). But the rule bodies also make
-- sharing, deferral and memoization decisions inline (V_DEFER, boxes and references, A8 free-
-- dimension cuts), and what a value is depends on the lazy-copy and substitution machinery above
-- being right. So the part that must be trusted is in effect this whole file. To make the
-- implementation's conformance to the published rules checkable by reading: the rules alone
-- determine results, and the machinery is confined to scheduling, sharing and identity, where by
-- construction (strong confluence of interaction nets; memo keys that are the full content of a
-- deterministic computation) it cannot change a result.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/prelude.cub): needs-work. CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, “Cubical Type Theory: a constructive interpretation of the univalence axiom”, arXiv:1611.02108) §7.1 “Composition for the universe” defines composition in the universe
-- as a Glue type along the equivalences given by transport (comp^i U [φ ↦ E] A0 = Glue [φ ↦ (E(i1),
-- equiv_i E(1−i))] A0). Here it is instead its own type former, %hcompU, with unglue on a face as
-- transport back along that face's line (%unglue-line) and its own composition (%comp-gluel,
-- %tproof): the design of Cubical Agda (Vezzosi, Mörtberg, Abel). The extra face ¬φ ↦ A0 is
-- admissible (on ¬φ the tube is empty and the composite is A0; φ ∧ ¬φ is 0 in the face lattice),
-- and makes a closed composition of types compute to a type. Not yet read against Cubical Agda's
-- published rules for this type former, its unglue and its composition; until then these four
-- clauses are trusted without a reference reading.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading the
-- evaluator's rule bodies (src/net.c's interaction cases, src/dnf.c's face algebra) side by side
-- with the published rules, CCHM (Cohen, Coquand, Huber, Mörtberg, TYPES 2015, LIPIcs 69) and the
-- clocked extension (Kristensen, Møgelberg, Vezzosi, arXiv:2102.01969), each rule recorded as
-- matching or deviating at its site. The two papers are not in the repository; fetch them. Until
-- this is done, conformance of the implementation to the calculus is not known.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading src/lang.c's
-- translation of terms into the evaluator's graph for anything that can change a term's meaning
-- (the strictness analysis that decides which operands are built eagerly, fully lazy lifting,
-- sweeping of compiled code, the module cache, whose key is the build date and the files' texts and
-- whose hits are trusted).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): question. Not yet done: reading
-- lib/check.cub's typing clauses against the calculus's typing rules, each recorded as matching or
-- deviating at its site.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The specializer carries its law in
-- its type, and the three Futamura projections hold (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6 “Reflective programming and language extension”: eq. (6.4) “the specialization equation”;
-- §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”: eqs. (6.6) “the first Futamura projection” and (6.7) “the second Futamura projection” and "any further projection"; §6.2 “Specialization as a program over the language’s own definitions”). The specializer
-- normalizes through a primitive that runs the untyped checker, so it cannot be specialized itself;
-- with normalization as a term (typed codes, the sibling node) it carries its law in its type and the three Futamura
-- projections follow. Specializing a process through a handler was measured superlinear
-- (lib/specialize.cub, 2026-11-09) and is unattributed. It waits on the typed codes above.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/specialize.cub): needs-work. Specialization cost: #build of a process
-- written through a handler was measured superlinear in the number of commands (2026-11-09: 7
-- commands 42M steps, 22 commands 1.21 billion steps, 3 min 39 s; experiments/os/build-cost*.cub
-- hold the per-stage probes). Not attributed (normalization, rewriting, or the passes of
-- lib/x86.cub). It is a cost of the language's own programs about programs.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/specialize.cub): needs-work. Normalization here is code-norm-at, a
-- primitive that runs the evaluator's own quoter (lib/std.cub's checker services). The
-- specializer is therefore not a term all the way down, and cannot be specialized itself: the
-- second and third Futamura projections (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §6.1 “Typed quotation, semantic readback, and self-application”: the second
-- is eq. (6.7) “the second Futamura projection”; "the same stage and representation indices apply to any further projection") need
-- normalization as a term of the language (normalization by evaluation over typed codes, with its
-- soundness equation, ibid. eq. (6.5) “reifySound”).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Native code replaces a definition only with the relation to its meaning
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9 “Native execution as a mathematical program transformation”). Native code today comes from an x86 translator whose output is bound and
-- run with no relation to the definition (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub), from pasted machine code
-- (lib/elf.cub), and with proofs about its bytes at one fixed placement (lib/compile/); nothing
-- relates a fleet's proved behaviour to its binaries (lib/deploy.cub). Decided by Avik
-- (2026-11-14): native realization goes through C. "are you going to complete writing proven-
-- correct x86 RISC ARM impls instead of writing a C impl ? the value delivered to me by a complete
-- x86 machine code impl is near-zero. cant even run on a macbook can it ... there remains no
-- argument for x86 as far as i can tell." One C emitter runs wherever a C compiler does; per-ISA
-- emitters and their proofs would multiply by every target. The paper permits it (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export": "C and C++ text are optional target presentations. If an external compiler produces the executed bytes,
-- either its relevant compilation relation is assumed or those bytes receive their own checked
-- connection"). What follows: the emitter returns the relation between running the emitted C and
-- the term's meaning, which needs the meaning of the C fragment the emitter produces (a small,
-- fixed subset), not of all of C (that stays language support); the C compiler's relation is a
-- named assumption listed by #trusted; the evaluator substitutes native code only under that
-- relation. The x86 translator (src/lang.c, lib/x86.cub, lib/machine.cub, lib/elf.cub's pasted
-- machine code, lib/compile/) is superseded by it. The record that preceded the decision: plan
-- revision 9 (archive/2026-11-11/PLAN-rev9.md, revision 9, 9 Nov 2026, sha256 4d012c3a10fd…, §5 “The distinctions everything above x86 rests on”, item 5 “The target is x86-64”) had chosen x86 ("Not LLVM IR,
-- not C"); a session on 2026-11-12 planned C to break a cycle in its goal graph; neither was
-- Avik's.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): wrong, against the paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…,
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export"): the
-- evaluator may replace a definition by native code only once a target-correctness witness relating
-- the code's execution to the definition exists. Here the bytes are bound after the only check
-- made, that the specialized term converts with the definition (lib/x86.cub jit-plan); the
-- translation from that term to bytes (x-push, x-lift, x-erase, the templates, this boundary's
-- marshaling) carries no relation, so a wrong translation would answer wrongly with nothing to
-- catch it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/deploy.cub): needs-work. fl-beh (what lib/fleet.cub's properties are
-- stated of) and fl-node (the process each binary runs) are both built from cp-view, but nothing
-- relates them: the properties proved of the behaviour are not shown to hold of the binaries. The
-- relation comes from processes realized as executables with their system-call events equal to the
-- process's interaction (native realization, above), applied here; nothing specific to
-- this file is owed.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/elf.cub): needs-work. The driver loop, the #build-loop round driver and
-- the start code below are machine code assembled from tools/asm/*.s and pasted as byte literals:
-- trusted as written, with no statement of what they do. They go when processes become native executables by the route
-- native realization takes (above).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/compile/meaning.cub): needs-work. This file, lib/compile/runs.cub,
-- lib/compile/tmpl.cub, lib/compile/process.cub's kernel (kn-step, a second kernel beside
-- lib/os/contract.cub's) and experiments/isa/addw-all.cub (one all-inputs theorem, for the bytes of
-- word addition) are proofs about the output of the x86 translator at one fixed placement, a form
-- the paper replaces (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”: Block, eq. (9.12);
-- seq, eq. (9.13); the compiler as a fold, eq. (9.15), with the relational congruence, eq. (9.16)). They are to be deleted with that
-- translator; the word, bit and flag law files beside them and x86ok's run lemmas remain
-- usable for x86 as a later target.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every law of the primitives is
-- proved, or named as an assumption (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, "Exact scalar
-- types"). lib/prim.cub postulates word and bit laws that
-- are provable from the unbounded definitions; fixed-width types have no specification; floating
-- point is trusted without a named assumption (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/prim.cub): needs-work. Of the 91 primitives here, the laws of word
-- multiplication, division, remainder and the bitwise operations, the wbit-* bit laws, the byte
-- load/store laws, array extensionality and the bit round trips are postulated, though words are
-- unbounded naturals and these laws are provable from the inductive definitions (lib/std.cub
-- already proves addition, equality, order, maximum and subtraction equal to theirs). The fixed-
-- width arithmetic (Int64, UInt32, UInt64) has no specification at all, and floating point is
-- wholly trusted. The paper (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, "Exact scalar types"):
-- a native primitive implements its definition under a checked theorem or a named assumption. Prove what is provable; define U_w = Fin(2^w) with its modular operations
-- and relate each fixed-width primitive to it or name the assumption; name IEEE binary64 as an
-- assumption. ---------------------------------------------------------------- the types
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Deriving is a typed term over the
-- code of a data declaration. lib/derive.cub's 24 definitions read the checker's own values and are
-- refused as untyped, so `deriving` is off and the Rust readers fail behind it. Deriving becomes a
-- term over the code of a data declaration, which needs a declaration's code as a value (work-as-a-
-- value). It waits on the work being a value (product implementation).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/derive.cub): needs-work. 24 definitions here are untyped and refused by
-- the checker, which has rejected untyped definitions outside the bootstrap files since 2026-11-09,
-- deliberately, after about 10,000 lines of untyped code were written by an agent. They are to be
-- written with types; nothing in the checker changes. `deriving eq` in the surface language depends
-- on this file and is off until it checks. 2026-11-12: they cannot be given types as they are: they
-- read the checker's own values (a constructor's telescope tx A nm r, tval, %var, %view (%gval g),
-- from lib/check.cub, a bootstrap file whose value types are not checked data), which no type of a
-- checked definition names. Typed, deriving is a term over a code of the data type's declaration
-- (its constructors and field types, as Code), not over the checker's values.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The evaluator handles terms of any
-- depth without quadratic cost or stack overflow. measured 2026-11-09, building a term cost
-- quadratic time in its depth and deep terms overflowed the C stack (src/net.c). Measured
-- 2026-11-13 at depths 2,000, 4,000 and 8,000: 2.6 s, 1.5 s, 4.0 s, so no longer quadratic there;
-- the stack at depth 10^6 is not re-measured.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): question. Measured 2026-11-09: build_m calls synkey at every
-- node it builds and synkey walks the whole subterm, so building a term of depth d costs d^2 key
-- computations (a unary numeral of 4*10^4 did not print in 260 s), and deep right-nested terms (a
-- numeral of 10^6) overflow the C stack in several recursive functions of this file and src/lang.c.
-- Not re-measured since; whether both still hold is not known.
-- Known (2026-11-14, at task-graph/hyperactivity.cub): needs-work. A file with one unbalanced
-- parenthesis in a definition (the graph's essential-context relation, one closing parenthesis
-- short) made the machine exit with status 1 and print nothing about it: no position, no reason. A
-- file that fails to read must say where and why.
-- Known (2026-11-14, at experiments/nbe/p26-wip.cub): needs-work. The library's typed transport
-- cannot be applied to a line of types that is a case on a path into Bool: false-true A e =
-- transport (λ i → if e i then A else (A → A)) (λ a → a) is refused with "an implicit argument is
-- not determined by the other arguments' types or the type expected", also with the family written
-- as a defined function by clauses (ft-fam). Writing the composition itself, comp (λ i → ft-fam A
-- (e i)) (λ i → []) (λ a → a), checks. So the elaborator does not find transport's implicit types
-- from a line it could read them from (its endpoints, the line at 0 and 1).
language-implementation-remainder : Node
language-implementation-remainder = node "Everything else the paper specifies for the language: the calculus conforming to its published rules, the specializer with its law, native realization, the laws of the primitives, deriving as a typed term, and terms of any depth." (cons language-implementation nil)
-- paper-examples (2026-11-13, Avik): "term-by-term construction of every single construction
-- including examples, appendix, in paper, as code in repo cited by content-address in paper ...
-- expressely de-prioritized ... while this *serves nothing*." It serves no node but the root, so
-- the order of work gives it nothing while anything else is open. The paper is Avik's current one;
-- the paper is the value hyperactivity (docs/hyperactivity.cub).
paper-examples : Node
paper-examples = node "Every construction in the paper, its examples and appendix included, as code in the repository, cited from the paper by content address." (cons the-product nil)

-- the-paper-is-one-value (2026-11-15, Avik): "The paper as pdf is ridiculous. It must be rendered
-- from code such that the information can be organized, parity can be ensured across dense/human
-- versions"; "Navigation of information space"; "ONE SOURCE OF INFORMATION THATS THE WHOLE DAMN
-- PROJECT"; "Nothing is ever allowed to be stale"; "somehow by construction we must ensure no pushes
-- violate this". Agreed with Avik (2026-11-15): the source holds each construction of the paper as
-- code (definition, statement, proof) with the English attached to what it explains; the pure page
-- (symbols, words only where they attach meaning) is the projection of the code and its naming
-- words, the human paper the projection of the code with all the attached English, navigation the
-- code's own dependencies, onboarding the walk of the same value by hash. No presentation is
-- written separately, so none can be stale. The work, in order: (1) the value holds the v32
-- manuscript (docs/Hyperactivity_Current_Draft.md, imported 2026-11-15) in its structure, and its
-- human projection reproduces that file byte for byte; (2) the stored copies (that file,
-- docs/hyperactivity_v31.md, docs/hyperactivity_v31-dense.md, docs/hyperactivity_v31.pdf,
-- docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf) leave the tree, kept in git history; (3) onboarding walks the
-- value; (4) the check refuses any push storing information twice, enforced at commit, at push and on
-- GitHub; (5) each construction's code attaches to it, growing the pure projection (paper-examples).
-- Done 2026-11-15: (1) lib/paper.cub (Block: heading, prose, construction, note; Paper with its front
-- matter; the projection `human`), docs/hyperactivity.cub (each of the 1052 blocks a definition named by
-- section and place, s1-1-p3; the value `hyperactivity`); `human hyperactivity` equals the v32
-- manuscript with runs of blank lines collapsed and a blank line after each heading, byte for byte.
-- Cost: checking the value fresh takes 170 s (about 190 machine steps per byte of text: each byte is
-- a cons the checker checks), once, since the store keeps each block under its content key (the graph
-- check now uses the store); rendering `human` takes about 100 s and is kept under the content it reads
-- (tools/task-graph.py documents). (2) the copies left the tree. (3) docs/onboarding.cub serves
-- `projection "the paper…" (human hyperactivity)` (lib/document.cub's second constructor).
-- (4) tools/task-graph.py one-source (no stored .pdf/.docx/.epub; no prose line of 100 characters or
-- more in two files), run by `hyperactive check`, .githooks/pre-commit and pre-push (core.hooksPath set
-- by every run of hyperactive) and .github/workflows/one-source.yml on every push and pull request.
-- GitHub.com runs no hook before accepting a push, so a push from a clone without the hooks is caught by
-- the GitHub check after it lands; requiring that check on main would refuse such pushes outright but
-- also every direct push, so it is not set. (5) begun 2026-11-15: lib/paper.cub's `realized` holds a
-- block and the names of the lib/ definitions that are its code; `pure` is the pure page (headings,
-- displayed constructions, the code realizing them; `code: NAME` lines, which the walk replaces by the
-- definition), and onboarding walks it (66k characters against the human projection's 335k);
-- `hyperactive paper [SECTION]` gives the human projection; the graph check fails on a name that is not
-- exactly one lib/ definition. Realized so far, from code already in lib/: (1.2) fiberEquiv;
-- §4.4's (4.17)–(4.27) from lib/sort.cub; (6.1) TCode, teval, (6.5) normalize-nf, normalize-sound,
-- (6.6) compiled, compiled-sound, (6.7) compiler-code, compiler-code-sound from lib/nbe.cub; §6.2's
-- specializer (lib/specialize.cub), §7.6's processes and handlers (lib/proc.cub, lib/world.cub,
-- lib/obs.cub), the fleet and its safety (lib/fleet.cub, §7.6, §10.10), C.2's mn-run (lib/compile/
-- meaning.cub). Written for the paper (2026-11-15): lib/observation.cub (§1–3: Warrants and refinement,
-- the reversible whole with no observed law, readings, le-trans, instances, the contractible
-- singleton) and lib/xor-language.cub (§5.6 whole: bytes as eight bits, the language, p0 and its
-- proof, annotation and erasure, cancel and its soundness, preserve, cancel p0 = Input) and §9.3 whole
-- in the same file (the byte-decoded stack machine, its instruction table, the compiler, sm-extends
-- (9.8) by structural induction, sm-program-halts (9.9), p0's bytes 01 02 5a 03 02 5a 03 ff halting
-- with the input, sm-transformed (9.10)). Found: the checker's inference of a path composition's
-- endpoints over machine states exhausts memory; giving them (sm-trans) checks at once. §10.3 whole in
-- lib/credit.cub (the specified state and total (10.6), allocation (10.7) and delivery (10.8) allowed
-- in the state they act on, cr-conserved over every allowed finite sequence, the observer's fiber
-- (10.9), receipt-bearing delivery with rcpt-conserved and rcpt-no-redelivery: once u is delivered no
-- allowed run delivers it again). §10.10's order ledger whole in lib/order-ledger.cub (R and I_B
-- (10.T1), the five transitions (10.T2), gw-safe (10.T3) from the initial state, J with dom(O) ⊆ U
-- preserved (10.T4) and gw-unique: count ≤ 1); a fill is proved to leave R at most unchanged, which
-- needs no key-uniqueness premise. §5.2 whole in lib/let-elimination.cub ((5.1)–(5.4) over de Bruijn
-- positions and total environments, the let-free core's substitution lemma, lx-D-forgets: D is not
-- an equivalence of syntax). Found: names are global across lib/ including lib/mem/ (rc-ids was
-- taken); the library check catches a collision. Then: lib/structure.cub (§1–§4: (1.5), (2.1), (2.2),
-- (2.7), (3.9)–(3.10), Vec ≃ (Fin n → A) (4.3)–(4.4), Monoid (4.6), transport of operation, predicate
-- and proof (4.7)–(4.12), (4.13)–(4.16), §4.4's sum trees with cost = Σ leaf depths and 8/9, 24/35);
-- lib/semiring-interp.cub ((6.11)–(6.13): homomorphisms commute with interpretation, over ring.cub's
-- polynomials); lib/source-semantics.cub (§5.5: D_Σ, the guarded unfolding, Language and meaning,
-- (5.11), the byte-memory instance whose execution is Read(a), Write(b, 0x6f), Done, (5.12)–(5.13a));
-- lib/behaviour.cub (truncation, HasCard, executions and Behavior, (8.2)–(8.5), (9.1), (9.17),
-- (9.C1), (9.C2)); lib/toy-isa.cub ((9.11), compileXor and its exact trace (9.14)); lib/maintenance.cub
-- ((10.2)–(10.5), (10.10)–(10.15), (10.17), (10.20), (10.21), §10.7, p₁ = x ⊕ 0x01);
-- lib/appendix-a.cub ((A.1), (A.2), (A.6)–(A.8)); existing core names attached where the paper
-- states them (refl, Path, transport, funExt, ua, uaβ, Bag, GProc, mach-gproc, mem-write, ...).
-- Avik, 2026-11-15: plenty more textual knowledge is to be encoded non-redundantly this way — about
-- 50 target documents for different purposes (neuroscience exposition, linguistics, AI alignment
-- publications, ...) of which many share content: the work is knowledge refactoring, lossless
-- compression into one source with each document a projection. Status record: PR #6.
-- Then (status record PR #6): words-bytes, frame-decoder, machines, behaviour, semantic-types,
-- interleavings (4.29), sum-edit, typed-language (definitions). Remaining, in order: §10.5's (10.19)
-- and inspect/plug, §9.2's (9.U2), (9.U2a), (9.U3), §7.6's Sim and ContBlock, (7.8), (6.2), (6.8), (6.9),
-- check, §6.5's example, (8.1), §8.5's U_w and (8.17); §5.1's lookup equation, binder law and
-- substitution theorem (an indexed eliminator on a constructor is a transport along refl: a path,
-- not a reduction, so each needs its computation lemma); Appendix C's 46 x86 bytes. Not library
-- code: the checker's own rules (1.1), (2.4)–(2.6), (3.3), (3.8), (3.12)–(3.14), and (A.3)–(A.5).
the-paper-is-one-value : Node
the-paper-is-one-value = node "The paper exists once, as a value of the language: its constructions as code with the English attached; every presentation of it is a projection of that value, and no push stores a second copy." (cons the-product nil)
-- paper-matches-implementation (2026-11-14, Avik): "All impl details in paper will be updated to match
-- all impl details in repo when impl is done. Use the work system to ensure this is so. Then don't
-- have to worry about that maximally uninteresting bullshit until everything interesting is done."
-- Every description in the paper of how something is implemented (e.g. its native-code section's
-- x86 encoder, mn-run and Correct1, which the C route replaces) is rewritten to what the repository
-- implements. It waits on the language implementation and the product implementation (after, below).
paper-matches-implementation : Node
paper-matches-implementation = node "Every implementation detail the paper states is updated to match the repository's implementation." (cons the-product nil)
-- product-implementation (2026-11-13, Avik): "all the product work of building a live interactive
-- collaboration system & tooling over the language & its terms". How it is held, changed and shared:
-- “State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md).
-- The product is not a system built on the language and has no parts: it is the language's own
-- notions taken seriously, one organism (Avik, 2026-11-13). A definition's identity is its content;
-- so a change is a new term, related to the old by a path or a square in the language; so nothing is
-- overwritten and there is no canonical state to merge into; so a view is a term the language
-- evaluates over the growing set of terms; and the interface is those views rendered for a person.
-- Each follows from the one before; none is built apart from the others.
-- What is known about this goal follows (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): facts,
-- not a breakdown and not parts; the session working on the goal decides how to break it down when its
-- work begins (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)).
-- Known (2026-11-13, Avik's correction): identity is semantic, two terms being the same exactly
-- when their content is; SHA-256 equality is one witness of it, not something interpretation
-- computes. Inside a run the evaluator's sound representation is interning (terms built from equal
-- parts share one node, so pointer equality is content identity, proved once about the interning);
-- a hash is needed only where identity crosses what a pointer cannot (another run, another machine,
-- the store), under collision resistance as a named assumption. So the findings below that ask for
-- every reference to be keyed by its content hash name the wrong fix: what is missing is
-- translating an evaluator result's run-local identity into a portable one when it leaves the run.
-- This is the paper's pattern throughout (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…: native code
-- §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, primitives §8.5 “Machine representations and memory laws”, digests §10.8 “Content-addressed definitions, mathematical identity, and a decentralized codebase”): a mechanism (native code, a primitive, a pointer, a hash) is
-- admitted only with its relation to the defined meaning.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the change-management reasoning): holding an object's content address
-- cannot be what grants seeing it, because the address of low-entropy content (a short message, a
-- small edit, a number) is guessable by hashing candidates; so visibility is decided where an
-- object crosses a boundary, the same place run-local identity becomes portable identity, and an
-- object whose audience is restricted gets a portable key that only its audience can compute, which
-- gives up sharing it with identical objects outside that audience and changes nothing about its
-- semantic identity.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work leaves git for the change
-- management Avik designed. The design is “State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md); his words are “To the coordinating session, on state, change and collaboration (2026-11-13, forwarded to this one)” (AI-AGENTS-WELCOME-TO-HYPERACTIVITY.md),
-- whose headings are cited here (they were sections 1 to 8 of docs/state-change-collaboration-design.md,
-- since folded into it): the work is a collection of immutable objects, not a tree of files ("Our
-- state is not text"); a session reads and writes through a query interface and materializes
-- locally only to act, an executable and its input ("A work session: read, write, act"); what a
-- session does internally stays invisible to Avik's views ("Visibility"); there is no canonical
-- state and nothing is promoted, every change being new code ("There is no canonical state", "Code
-- is immutable and content-addressed"); old objects are marked dead by a statement with a reason
-- ("Old objects, and marking them dead"); a change to an inner construction relates its users' old
-- and new versions by a square ("Change is a square"); questions are tasks with reasons ("Questions
-- are tasks"). Git and Pijul are the wrong base ("Code is immutable and content-addressed"). Until it exists, git carries the work: one session, on
-- main only, no branch, every change pushed the moment it is made (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)).
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): Every definition and every result
-- is identified by its content and kept, so work is shared across runs and collaborators.
-- definitions and results identified by content, the foundation of collaboration with no canonical
-- state (“State, change and collaboration” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). A reference is keyed by its index in the run
-- (src/net.c), so no result is kept or shared beyond a run (src/lang.c); the checker's identity is
-- a hash of file texts (src/store.c); the store writes in place, so a killed machine leaves a
-- truncated entry (src/store.c); the standard commands never use the store (Makefile). Elaborations
-- are already remembered (a warm library check, 4.6 s). It also makes every check fast, which
-- speeds all other work.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Every evaluator
-- result read back here (checks, the elaborator's solving rounds via %chk-def / %infer-ok, usages,
-- readings) lives only for this run. Each is to be kept beyond it in canonical form: the query
-- identified by its canonical key (references as content keys; the checker identified by its
-- own definitions' keys), the result stored with references as keys and rendered through the
-- current bindings when read, the same mechanism as the store's definitions. The store's
-- certificates are then such results; the per-file cache goes (gone 2026-11-12). Read 2026-11-12:
-- the query built here (build_top) is keyed in src/net.c by synkey, but that key's leaves are run-
-- local: a definition by its global index (key_global), a λ or thunk by the address of its compiled
-- record (K_LAM over t->rec), and the codes passed name checked definitions by their index (cglob
-- i). So the evaluator's key of a query means nothing in another run. Two key schemes exist: the
-- evaluator's (structural, run-local) and the store's (canonical forms, content keys). The end
-- state is one: every key canonical, compiled records keyed by their content. The first step
-- can be taken here: the query's key formed as the store forms a definition's (its canonical form,
-- references as content keys, plus the checker's identity), and the result stored canonically.
-- 2026-11-12: one kind of result is kept so: a declaration's whole elaboration (src/syntax.c,
-- x_checked; below, done). The evaluator's own results, %chk-def's and the readings', are not yet.
-- Read 2026-11-12, for the elaborator's case (src/syntax.c x_checked, the round loop): the right
-- unit is the whole elaboration of a declaration, its input the declaration's surface forms (sig,
-- body) with every name resolved to the key it denotes, its output the solved core forms T and t.
-- The final forms then go through `checked` as now, so the certificate check (key_def,
-- store_checked) still decides acceptance: a remembered elaboration can only change which term is
-- checked, never whether a term is accepted. What is missing is a faithful canonical serialization:
-- canon (src/ store.c) is a key, not a serialization. It drops the type written on a λ's binder
-- (bnd_ty, kept on the name node), instance flags and binder names, and prints a λ's binders so
-- their count cannot be read back. The store needs one canonical form that is both the key and
-- readable back (references as keys, binders nameless but counted, binder types and flags kept),
-- rendered through the current bindings when read; with it canon is that form's hash and nothing is
-- duplicated. Done 2026-11-12 (branch product-implementation, merged): canon's binders readable
-- back and decanon (every library definition round-trips); generated names numbered by first
-- occurrence in an elaboration's key and in its stored form, binders' kept names included (x-case-
-- chain had failed warm, "rec of a name that is not a recursive field of its branch", its pattern's
-- field renumbered while the rebuilt scrutinee's let kept the raw name that `rec` resolves); data
-- keys nominal; the elaboration remembered. Acceptance is not remembered with it: a
-- remembered elaboration is a term like any other, accepted by its own key's certificate or by the
-- checker (the first version marked a hit as already checked, which would have accepted a stale
-- elaboration unchecked). Measured, the library with the store warm and the per-file cache cold:
-- 4.2 s of CPU, no evaluator step, the same 1907 passed and 52 failed as cold (29 s before).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/net.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). A reference to a
-- definition is keyed by its index in the current run, so no key involving one means the same thing
-- in another run, and no evaluator result mentioning a definition can be remembered beyond the run.
-- The key of a reference is to be the definition's content key (src/store.c's key of its canonical
-- form); equal content is equal meaning, so this is sound for values. Results that name definitions
-- (readback text, elaboration solutions) are then stored canonically and rendered through current
-- bindings. 2026-11-12: a λ or thunk is likewise keyed by the address of its compiled record
-- (synkey, K_LAM over t->rec), so compiled records need content keys too for any key to be
-- canonical.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/store.c): the checker's identity here is the hash of the texts of the
-- files loaded up to the checker's definition (comments and unrelated definitions included) plus
-- the machine binary, not the content keys of the definitions the checker consists of. So a comment
-- edited in the bootstrap files invalidates every certificate in the store.
-- Known (2026-11-13, at src/store.c): wrong. store_write writes an entry in place (fopen "w"). A
-- machine killed while writing (measured 2026-11-13: the kernel killed one for lack of memory while
-- four read in parallel) leaves a truncated entry that later runs read as a whole one, and two
-- machines on one store can read each other's half-written entries; the store is a cache whose
-- entries decide what is not checked again, so a truncated entry could change a result. Fix: write
-- to a temporary file in the same directory and rename it over the entry (atomic on POSIX). Until
-- then tools/task- graph.py gives each parallel reader its own store.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at Makefile): every target sets CUBICAL_NOCACHE=1, so the standard commands
-- never use the store and recheck everything from nothing (64 s for the library). With the store
-- warm, elaboration and compilation still run for every definition (29 s), so incremental checking
-- reaches only the checker's run, not the rest of the work done per definition. Measured again
-- 2026-11-12, the elaboration and the checker's answers (accepted or refused) kept in the store,
-- the per-file cache gone: with the store warm the library takes 4.6 s, its output the fresh run's
-- but for step counts; what remains per run is compilation (compiled records keyed by content,
--). The targets still check from nothing.
-- Known (2026-11-13, from the review of every earlier finding): The work is a value of the
-- language: every declaration's code and reason readable by views, and the interface reads it.
-- serve mode gives views every declaration of a type as a value, but not each declaration's code
-- (src/lang.c); most declarations have no recorded reason (lib/task- graph.cub). The interface for
-- Avik (no editor, no IDE) reads the work through it.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at src/lang.c): needs-work (goal product-implementation). Serve mode is how
-- agents and people use the work continuously. Missing: the work given to a term as a value (the
-- declarations and their bindings, each declaration's code), so that views are terms of the
-- language applied to it, and a reading that evaluates a view. Replacing a binding is #retract then
-- a new declaration. 2026-11-12: `declarations T` is every checked definition of type
-- T in scope, as a list (src/ syntax.c), so views over values of a type are terms (lib/task-
-- graph.cub's over Node) and `#show` of one is the reading that evaluates it (tools/task-graph.py
-- view). Still missing: each declaration's code as a value, so that a view can read the code
-- itself, every declaration of every type.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/task-graph.cub): The work as one graph of nodes. Done: goals and
-- tickets are one kind of node (task-graph/hyperactivity.cub); every declaration of the code is a
-- node of the same graph, its reason computed from the machine's reading `#uses` (a declaration
-- with a reason mentions it), from the machine naming it (src/), or stated in the comment above it
-- as `(part of NODE)` or `(prerequisite to NODE)` (tools/task-graph.py why, unexplained). Measured
-- 2026-11-12 over every file: 4310 of 8312 declarations have no reason; stating the rest where the
-- code's text establishes a reason is what remains (“The Hyperactive Workflow” (docs/to-be-incorporated-into-paper.md)). Library
-- API nothing uses (lib/std.cub's pow, fact, happly, the univalence lemmas; lib/prelude.cub's
-- untyped helpers) states none: whether each stays is open.
-- Known (2026-11-14, Avik): `hyperactive` is the primary work interface and is built first, since it
-- accelerates all further work; the README holds only setup and hands off to it. Done (2026-11-14):
-- `hyperactive` with no argument begins the walk (now over the value onboarding, docs/onboarding.cub,
-- below); `hyperactive def NAME` reads a definition by name with its statement and what uses it, with
-- no file or position needed. Not yet: writing through `hyperactive`, code read only by query (the
-- walk and def still print file paths), and onboarding into the core code.
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): the screen shows information, and information acts: it acts on the user
-- through the senses and guides attention, and the user acts on the device through its senses; two
-- directions of one flow. The loop is real time, not turns ("Consciousness is one continuous loop
-- with no turns"). Messaging is out. Upon apparent disagreement the system presents the basis of
-- agreement: the shared fundamental values, and where inference over them diverges by experience;
-- what is sought is the most intelligent consensus available, users developing shared knowledge and
-- thinking individually and together (docs/information-acts-and-common-ground.md and
-- docs/eye-contact-co-processing-at-the-speed-of-light.md, his words).
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): code does not live in files. When the store is proper, no .cub file
-- exists: the language and all code are objects in the store, referred to by hash. The system does
-- not rely on the filesystem ontology and rejects it for computing (docs/no-filesystem.md, his
-- words). This is the design already stated in “State, change and collaboration”: the work is a
-- collection of immutable objects, not a tree of files ("Our state is not text"). Today every piece
-- of code is a file and refers to others by path (import, document, text); that is the git stand-in,
-- which the store replaces, not something to be edited path by path.
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): the bootstrap from the filesystem is one reader and only one: it computes
-- the hash of every file; the hash is the reference. "Unison certainly solved this already": its
-- code refers to definitions only by hash, and names are a separate mapping from names to hashes.
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): the design of onboarding and of the paper delta. Everything in the
-- repository is code = data: hyperactivity is built with hyperactivity. Existing text files keep
-- their own extensions (.txt, .md) and are loaded by path as raw content: a string value whose bytes
-- are exactly the file's (no escaping, no new extension, so Avik's messages paste in as they are);
-- an essay is one string. The paper delta is a set of
-- pointers to essay files and nothing else (no descriptions), as a value in the language; it is not
-- a living notes document. Onboarding forces reading the paper and the essays: the CLI serves one
-- chunk at a time, small enough never to be truncated by an agent's harness, with only next and prev
-- (no jumping), as the only way the content is served; reading is the tokens entering the agent's
-- context, so nothing more (no passphrase) is needed, and re-walking restores what compaction
-- evicted. Done (2026-11-15; the child onboarding-from-the-paper-delta): `text "path"`
-- (src/syntax.c) loads an existing file as raw content; docs/paper-delta.cub is `paper-delta : List
-- Document`, four `document "path"` pointers (path and content hash) and its first line the check
-- against v31; docs/onboarding.cub is `onboarding : List Document` (AGENTS.md, welcome file, the paper (the
-- editable source docs/Hyperactivity_Current_Draft.md when present, else v31 labelled historical), Labs
-- note, the delta's pieces, word list, etymologies); `hyperactive` begins the walk over its pointers,
-- `hyperactive next` and `hyperactive prev` move one chunk of at most 8000 characters cut at
-- paragraphs (the paper's at its sections), the position kept in build/; the task graph and what
-- can be taken up follow, generated. The numbered `walk N` is gone. Corrected (2026-11-15, Avik:
-- "Either it is purely correct code ... or it's wrong"): the walk takes its documents from the
-- machine's evaluation of onboarding (tools/task-graph.py documents), not from the file's text, and
-- serves a file only when its SHA-256 is the digest the value holds; a Document's path is from the
-- repository root (src/syntax.c), so the value names its files on its own; the root's essential
-- context is the value onboarding itself, so the graph's check checks it; the PDF is served as
-- what the value points to, labelled a snapshot, never silently replaced by another file.
-- Done (2026-11-14, Avik: "an immediate invaluable tool"; source replaced 2026-11-14, Avik: "Absolute
-- total coverage up to modernity"): `hyperactive etym TEXT` returns any text with every word augmented
-- by its etymology, so agents read Avik's words in the meanings the words carry. Source: English
-- Wiktionary (snapshot 2026-10-01, CC BY-SA 4.0) as rendered in the Wikimedia Enterprise HTML dumps,
-- packaged per language by xxyzz/wiktionary_stardict (GitHub release 20261029), fetched once into
-- build/ and indexed into build/etym.sqlite: 450,125 of 817,247 English headwords have an etymology,
-- coinages to the present included (empathy, genocide, apoptosis, neurotypical); each origin is
-- followed through 50 other languages' entries (Latin, Ancient Greek, French, Old English, Arabic …),
-- terms in their own script with transliteration and gloss; 200,829 chains end at a
-- Proto-Indo-European root, 257,930 at some reconstructed root. GCIDE (Webster's 1913) is the
-- fallback for the 9,280 words only it has. Not yet: Wiktionary's reconstruction pages (not in the
-- dumps; a root's sense is the gloss its citing entries give), so chains stop where a reconstruction
-- page would continue them (Proto-Hellenic *génos); a word's later etymology paragraphs (an
-- alternative or deeper root given there) are shown but not followed; kaikki.org (wiktextract) and
-- dumps.wikimedia.org are unreachable from these sessions.
-- Known (2026-11-14, at tools/task-graph.py): needs-work. `hyperactive why` and `hyperactive
-- unexplained` read every file through the machine before answering, and took over ten minutes
-- without finishing on 2026-11-14, so the check that every top-level object of the code has a reason
-- is not usable from the interface.
product-implementation : Node
product-implementation = node "The live interactive collaboration system and tooling over the language and its code and data." (cons the-product nil)
-- onboarding-from-the-paper-delta (2026-11-15, Avik: "onboarding should force reading paper &
-- essays"; "set of pointers to file"): split from product-implementation when its work began. The
-- design and what was done are the 2026-11-15 facts in product-implementation's statement.
-- Known (2026-11-15, Avik): not done, and nothing blocks it. Onboarding is files pointing to files
-- by path; in the design the paper delta and the onboarding order hold hashes and the walk serves
-- objects of the store by hash (product-implementation's statement). The store exists
-- (.cubical-store, src/store.c: entries under the SHA-256 of their content, and a names log of
-- name, key and time); what onboarding needs of it (a file's bytes kept under their hash) is a step
-- of this work, not a prerequisite.
-- Known (2026-11-15, measured): the walk is 749k characters, about 190k tokens, more than one
-- agent's working context, so an agent compacts before finishing it. Shares: paper v31 337k, the
-- welcome file 174k, to-be-incorporated-into-paper 88k, Hyperactive_Labs 39k, the firm outline 35k.
-- Avik (2026-11-15): there is a knowledge engineering work here; he hates having to reeducate. The
-- paper exposes and raises everything for a human reader; the AI reader gets a much denser knowledge
-- product, written for a reader perfectly fluent in 2025 computer science (named known work, e.g.
-- the Futamura projections, is a pointer, not exposition), lossless, possibly beyond 70%
-- compaction; his own way of delivering insight to agents is that form, and it fits any context.
-- The dense form (2026-11-15, Avik: "sentence by sentence paragraph by paragraph lossless
-- densification for expert readers"): written in full from v31's text, 216k characters against
-- 314k, 31% smaller, because it kept statements of what a construction is not or does not imply,
-- which carry no further truth content (Avik). It was a separately written presentation, so it left
-- the tree with the one-source paper (the-paper-is-one-value); git history keeps it.
-- Avik (2026-11-15, on the dense form): "The paper as pdf is ridiculous. It must be rendered from code
-- such that the information can be organized, parity can be ensured across dense/human versions";
-- English thinking is structured, relational and rational and should be expressed as such: navigation
-- of information space. The 31% result's stated reasons (statements of what a construction is not or
-- does not imply) carry no further truth content and should have been compacted away; "maybe the
-- paper should be a .cub file with modes"; a pure proof page is mathematical symbols, with words only
-- where essential to attach meaning.
onboarding-from-the-paper-delta : Node
onboarding-from-the-paper-delta = node "Onboarding serves the paper and the essays through hyperactive, from the paper delta, a value of the language holding pointers to essay files." (cons product-implementation nil)
-- history-of-the-work (2026-11-15, Avik: "hyperactivity history should enter history explorer in the
-- product"; "near zero machinery"; split from product-implementation when its work began): his words
-- and what follows from the design are docs/the-history-of-the-work.md. Known (2026-11-15, Avik:
-- "python tool is out. ... this is a meta-circular loop"): the history is code of the language, its
-- views evaluated by the machine over the work's own values, as the task graph's views are; a Python
-- reading of git (tools/history.py, built and removed the same day) is not it.
history-of-the-work : Node
history-of-the-work = node "The history of the work, read from git into the store and navigable from any object as what changed there, with no model's judgement when it is read." (cons product-implementation nil)
-- product-implementation-remainder (2026-11-15, split from product-implementation with onboarding): what product-implementation's statement holds beyond onboarding.
product-implementation-remainder : Node
product-implementation-remainder = node "Everything else the product implementation needs." (cons product-implementation nil)
-- language-support (2026-11-13, Avik): "Languages support (semantics/syntax as terms for particular
-- languages) * RISC x86, C++, C, Rust, OCaml, Python etc". "before we start any such work, certain
-- things need to be at perfection": it waits on language-implementation and product-implementation
-- (after, below).
-- Known (2026-11-12, at experiments/isa/build-run.cub): needs-work. The imported x86 model has no
-- translation of the two-byte opcode 0F AE (mfence and its group), an inventory gap of the Sail
-- import; any binary using it stops there under the model.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/isa/tso.cub): question. TsoSound is stated and has no inhabitant: no
-- definition proves it. The litmus files in experiments/tso establish instances by computation for
-- particular threads and bounded schedules only. Likewise AtomsX86Correct in lib/mem/rc11.cub.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. The C++ reading has drifted from what it
-- stands on, measured 2026-11-12 with each file loaded alone after its imports (--read): here 21
-- failures (type mismatches at lines 64 and 78 and others; a primitive declared outside the
-- bootstrap files, refused); lib/cxx/read-expr.cub 70 (its cases miss constructors lib/cxx/ast.cub
-- gained later: ce-string- literal-units, ck-FloatingCast, clayout-v, ctk-preferred-alignof);
-- experiments/mem/sum.cub (a record field mem-allocs and ev-run-pure, ev-value that no longer
-- exist). The renaming of lib/mem/scalar.cub's operators to ao-... had not reached these files or
-- lib/lang/rust/read- rvalue.cub; done 2026-11-12. The readers are to be brought to the AST and the
-- memory as they are.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/machine.cub): wrong. Measured 2026-11-11 (memory files, then this
-- file and read-expr.cub, after the library): this file 21 failures, read-expr.cub 70. Roots: cx-
-- unsupported (here) and cx-run (below) do not check against CxEv's type; the primitive cx-byte is
-- refused (primitives exist only in the bootstrap files); the rest follow from these (cx-sk, cx-
-- size undefined; implicit arguments applied to functions without them). The C++ reading does not
-- check as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/cxx/read-expr.cub): wrong. This file refers to read.cub (statements,
-- declarations, the fuel that ties calls) for meanings it does not define; no lib/cxx/read.cub
-- exists. The C++ reading is incomplete as it stands.
-- Known (2026-11-12, at lib/lang/rust/machine.cub): needs-work. Measured 2026-11-11: these deriving
-- lines fail because lib/derive.cub, which deriving needs, is refused as untyped; the Rust
-- readers then fail on what follows (machine 6, read-place 21, read-rvalue 26, read 22 failures).
-- Typing derive.cub repairs the root.
language-support : Node
language-support = node "The syntax and semantics of particular languages as code of the language: x86, C, C++, Rust, OCaml, Python and others." (cons the-product nil)
